  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a convex relaxation approach for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The problem is known to be non-convex due to the cross-correlation term, which complicates finding a global optimum. Our strategy relaxes the non-convex constraints into a convex form via second-order cone programming (SOCP) and uses warm-starting to leverage the initial_h_values. This approach aims to escape poor local minima by solving a series of convex subproblems.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 \u211d^n_points, h\u0303 = (1,h) for boundary handling\n- Constraints:\n  - h \u2208 [0,1] for all i\n  - \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1 \u2192 sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = ||h * h\u0303||\u2081 * dx, where * is cross-correlation\n- Relaxation: The problem becomes convex by enforcing h\u0303 \u2ab0 0 via SOCP\n- Formulation:\n  We minimize C\u2085 subject to:\n  1. 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n  2. sum(h) = n_points / 2\n  3. The correlation matrix A satisfies A * h \u2264 C\u2085 * b, where b handles boundary effects\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. CVXPY formulation:\n   - Use MOSEK or ECOS solver\n   - Define h as variable with bounds [0,1]\n   - Set up SOCP constraints using the correlation structure\nb. Initialization:\n   - Use initial_h_values if available, else create a symmetric feasible solution\nc. Optimization loop:\n   1. Solve the relaxed SOCP problem\n   2. Extract h from dual variables\n   3. Project h onto [0,1] and scale to meet sum constraint\n   4. Validate against original evaluator\nd. Rounding procedure:\n   - Soft thresholding with exponential decay to enforce boundaries\n   - Final projection: h = clip(rescale(h), 0, 1) * 2 / n_points\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- n_points: 100 (matches initial_h_values)\n- Initial scaling factor: 1.1 (adjust as needed)\n- Rounding threshold decay: exponential, tuned for boundary effects\n- Boundary handling: explicit correlation matrix for A and b\n- Warm-start: initial_h_values used to center the search\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- SOCP solve with CVXPY: single solve within budget\n- Projecting the solution: O(n_points)\n- Worst-case time: <600s even with solver overhead\n- Robust fallback to initial_h_values if necessary\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Validate feasibility of proposed h before returning\n- Exactly check sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Compute C\u2085 via evaluator to confirm consistency\n- Return: h, C\u2085, and n_points following all task constraints\n- Small n_points test (n=10) included for debugging",
